he state should be OBSERVATION_SEEN or FINAL_SEEN. If state is THOUGHT_SEEN or ACTION_SEEN, that means incomplete cycle at end. In that case we could set valid = False and first_violation_line = len(lines) (the last line) and valid_prefix = records[:-1]? But that's tricky. Since we don't have a clear spec, I'll assume input will always be either valid or have a clear mid-sequence violation. I'll implement the check: after loop, if state is THOUGHT_SEEN or ACTION_SEEN (or ACTION_INPUT_SEEN if we had that sub-state), then set valid=False, first_violation_line = len(lines) (the last line), valid_prefix = records (all records). But that doesn't match \"valid prefix\" concept. Alternatively, treat the violation as occurring after the last line, but we can't return a line number. I think it's safer to not add such validation and rely on the input having actual violations within lines. The fixture does not include incomplete cycles. I'll leave it out but note that the spec says \"Parse only complete ... records\", so if a record is incomplete (e.g., Action without Observation), it's a violation. But if we only have lines, an Action line is a complete record? The record is a line, but the cycle is incomplete. The spec says \"valid_turn_order\" and \"require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So an incomplete cycle violates turn order. The violation occurs at the line that breaks the order? Actually if we have Thought, Action, and then no more lines, the violation is that we didn't get Observation. The first violation is the absence of Observation. But there is no line. So likely the problem expects that the input will always either be fully valid or have a line that directly violates the expected pattern (like a Thought after Action). So I'll only detect violations based on line content vs expected state. At the end, if no violation, I'll set valid=True and first_violation_line=None. That should be fine for given cases.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Parse line: split on first colon. type = part before colon stripped, content = part after colon stripped. If no colon, invalid line. If type not in {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Action Input\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}, invalid.\n\n- State machine states: \"START\", \"THOUGHT\", \"ACTION\", \"ACTION_INPUT\", \"OBSERVATION\", \"FINAL\". Transitions:\n  - START: accept \"Thought\" -> go THOUGHT, else violation.\n  - THOUGHT: accept \"Action\" -> go ACTION, else violation.\n  - ACTION: accept \"Action Input\" -> go ACTION_INPUT, OR \"Observation\" -> go OBSERVATION, else violation.\n  - ACTION_INPUT: accept \"Observation\" -> go OBSERVATION, else violation.\n  - OBSERVATION: accept \"Thought\" -> go THOUGHT, OR \"Final\" -> go FINAL, else violation. But note: after Observation, only Thought or Final. If we get another Observation, violation. Also if we get Action, violation.\n  - FINAL: no further lines allowed; if any line appears after Final, violation. But we can detect this by checking that state is FINAL before processing next line. Or at end we must have no more lines.\n\n- Also keep track if we have seen Final: once we see Final, set seen_final = True. Then any subsequent line violation regardless of type.\n\n - For Final, only allowed as last line. But we can't know if it's last until we finish. So we allow Final only if it's the last line. But we can check after processing: if we processed a Final and there are more lines, then the line after Final is violation. So we can handle: when we see Final, we set state = FINAL. Then for any subsequent line, violation (first_violation_line = that line). That works.\n\n- Need to record each parsed record as a dict with \"type\" (lowercase) and \"content\" (string). Also possibly include line number? Not required for output but we can include in records? The spec says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records\" so likely just list of {type, content}.\n\n- Output dict: {\"valid\": bool, \"records\": list, \"first_violation_line\": int or None, \"valid_prefix\": list}. The valid_prefix should be the records up to but not including the violation line. If no violation, valid_prefix = records.\n\n- Edge cases: empty lines list? Then no records, valid? Possibly not, because we need at least one Thought? But spec likely expects non-empty. We'll treat empty as invalid? The first violation would be line 1? But there is no line. Maybe raise ValueError? The task says raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. But empty lines is structurally invalid. I'll raise ValueError.\n\n- Also note: the case payload has \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore.\n\nImplement function solve(case) that returns a dict. Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the JSON output. Ensure implementation source string is properly escaped.\n\nI'll write the Python code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: state machine handling of Action Input. In example they don't have Action Input, but we should support it. Also, what about multiple consecutive Thoughts? The state machine from THOUGHT only accepts Action, so second Thought would be violation. That matches \"invalid order\" example.\n\nNow write code.\n\nWe'll include docstring? Not required, but fine.\n\nAlso need to handle case where line is exactly \"Final: done\" etc. Content may have leading/trailing spaces, strip.\n\nImplement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case[\"lines\"]\n    if not lines:\n        raise ValueError(\"Empty lines\")\n    records = []\n    state = \"START\"\n    seen_final = False\n    violation_line = None\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        # parse line\n        parts = line.split(\":\", 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            # invalid line format\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        typ = parts[0].strip()\n        content = parts[1].strip()\n        if typ not in (\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Action Input\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"):\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        # Check state transitions\n        if seen_final:\n            # after final, any line is violation\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        valid_transition = False\n        if state == \"START\":\n            if typ == \"Thought\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"THOUGHT\"\n        elif state == \"THOUGHT\":\n            if typ == \"Action\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"ACTION\"\n        elif state == \"ACTION\":\n            if typ == \"Action Input\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"ACTION_INPUT\"\n            elif typ == \"Observation\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"OBSERVATION\"\n        elif state == \"ACTION_INPUT\":\n            if typ == \"Observation\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"OBSERVATION\"\n        elif state == \"OBSERVATION\":\n            if typ == \"Thought\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"THOUGHT\"\n            elif typ == \"Final\":\n                valid_transition = True\n                state = \"FINAL\"\n                seen_final = True  # but we need to check later if it's last\n        elif state == \"FINAL\":\n            # shouldn't happen as seen_final catches before, but just in case\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        if not valid_transition:\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        # Record\n        records.append({\"type\": typ.lower(), \"content\": content})\n        # After processing Final, we must ensure no more lines; we already set seen_final, so next iteration will break if there is next line.\n        # But if it's the last line, it's fine.\n    # After loop, check for violation\n    if violation_line is not None:\n        # Return valid_prefix = records before this line (i.e., up to violation_line-1)\n        valid_prefix = records[:violation_line - 1] if violation_line > 1 else []\n        return {\n            \"valid\": False,\n            \"records\": records,  # all parsed up to violation? spec says \"return lowercase typed trajectory records\" - maybe all records including the invalid? But better to include only valid ones? The spec: \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" Possibly should include all parsed until violation? I'll include all in 'records' field. But the violation line itself is not a valid record? Actually we parsed it and found it invalid, so we did not add it to records. So records only contain valid ones. That's fine.\n            \"first_violation_line\": violation_line,\n            \"valid_prefix\": valid_prefix\n        }\n    else:\n        # Check if final state is acceptable: must be either OBSERVATION or FINAL. If not, that's a violation at the last line.\n        if state not in (\"OBSERVATION\", \"FINAL\"):\n            # incomplete cycle at end\n            # treat last line as violation? Actually the last line is Thought or Action (valid record) but after it, expected next record missing.\n            # We'll set violation_line to len(lines) and valid_prefix = records[:-1] (excluding last record)\n            # But spec says \"on the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" The violation is after the last line? Hard.\n            # Since the spec doesn't cover this, I'll treat it as invalid with first_violation_line = len(lines) and valid_prefix = records[:-1].\n            # However, in practice, we might not need this because input always ends with Observation or Final. I'll include it for completeness.\n            if records:\n                valid_prefix = records[:-1]\n                violation_line = len(lines)\n            else:\n                valid_prefix = []\n                violation_line = 1  # empty?\n            return {\n                \"valid\": False,\n                \"records\": records,\n                \"first_violation_line\": violation_line,\n                \"valid_prefix\": valid_prefix\n            }\n        return {\n            \"valid\": True,\n            \"records\": records,\n            \"first_violation_line\": None,\n            \"valid_prefix\": records\n        }\n\nBut wait: what about the case where we have a Final that is not the last line? Our state machine will allow Final but then we set seen_final=True. Then the next line will trigger violation because seen_final is True. That will correctly set violation_line to the line after Final. But the Final itself is a valid record (if it's the last line, it's fine). However, if Final appears in the middle and then a line follows, our code will break at the following line. But the violation should be at the Final line itself? The spec says \"Permit Final only at the end\". So the violation is that Final is not at the end. The first violation is the Final line itself? Actually if you have Final at line 4 and then another line at line 5, the first violation is at line 4 because Final is not allowed there. So we need to detect that when we encounter Final, if it's not the last line, it's a violation. But we don't know if it's the last line at that moment. We could wait until we see a subsequent line, but then the violation line would be line 5, but the problem is with Final at line 4. The spec says \"on the first violation return its one-based line\". Which line is the first violation? Final being not at end is a violation that occurs at the point of Final, but it's not invalid until we see a subsequent line. Alternatively, we can consider that Final appearing not at end is an immediate violation because it violates stop semantics. So we should detect that when we see \"Final\" and state is OBSERVATION (valid transition), we must check if this is the last line. But we don't know. So we can defer the check: after processing all lines, if Final was encountered but not at last line, then the first violation line is the line of Final. So we need to track if we saw Final and its line number. Then after loop, if not violation_line already set, check if seen_final and its line number < len(lines). Then set violation_line = final_line, and valid_prefix = records[:final_line-1] (since final record itself is invalid). Then return valid=False.\n\nSimilarly, if we end in incomplete state (not OBSERVATION/FINAL), set violation_line to last line (since it's the incomplete record? But that record is valid as a record, but the cycle is incomplete. The spec says \"require ... for each nonterminal cycle\" so if the last record is a Thought without subsequent Action, that's a violation of turn order at the last line? The last line itself is a Thought, which is a valid record, but the violation is that after it, we needed Action. So the first violation is the missing Action, which doesn't have a line. Could arguably be the last line because that's where the cycle broke. I think it's safer to assume input will not end in incomplete cycle because it would be caught earlier? Actually we need to consider: if input ends with Thought, the last line is a Thought, which is valid under the state machine from OBSERVATION, but then we stop. The spec says \"Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle\" - so if we end with Thought, that's an incomplete cycle. That is a violation. The first violation should be the line where the cycle broke? The Thought is valid, but the violation is that the next expected record (Action) is missing. Since we don't have that line, we might treat the violation as occurring after the last line. But we cannot return a line number. I'll skip this case and rely on the fact that the given cases will have explicit violations or be fully valid. To be thorough, I'll add a check for final state and treat it as violation at the last line (since the last record is the one that started the incomplete cycle). That would be line len(lines). But then the valid_prefix would be records[:-1]. However, that excludes the last record, which is itself valid? But the violation is that the cycle is incomplete, so the last record is part of the violation? It might be considered that the entire cycle is invalid. But the spec says \"on the first violation return ... valid prefix\" - the valid prefix should be all records that are part of valid cycles. If the last line is a Thought that starts a cycle, that cycle is incomplete, so it's not valid. So we should not include it in valid prefix. Thus valid_prefix = records[:-1] makes sense. The first_violation_line would be the line of that record (the last line). That seems plausible. We'll implement that.\n\nBut the simpler approach: only detect violations based on line-by-line state machine and also check Final position after processing all lines. For incomplete ending, treat as violation at last line.\n\nNow incorporate these checks after the main loop.\n\nAlso, we should consider that Action Input could be multiple? No, only one per action.\n\nLet's write final code.\n\nWe'll also handle case where there are no lines: raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\nNote: The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source. The source must be properly escaped: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". Use a Python string in JSON.\n\nI'll write the code and then dump to JSON using a small script in my mind, but I'll manually escape.\n\nBetter to just write the source as a multi-line string and then escape manually.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\n    \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case[\\\"lines\\\"]\\n    if not lines:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Empty lines\\\")\\n    records = []\\n    state = \\\"START\\\"\\n    final_line = None\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\\n        # parse\\n        if \\\":\\\" not in line:\\n            first_violation = i\\n            break\\n        typ, _, content = line.partition(\\\":\\\")\\n        typ = typ.strip()\\n        content = content.strip()\\n        valid_types = {\\\"Thought\\\", \\\"Action\\\", \\\"Action Input\\\", \\\"Observation\\\", \\\"Final\\\"}\\n        if typ not in valid_types:\\n            first_violation = i\\n            break\\n        # ... continue ...\n}\n\nBut need to track first_violation variable. I'll implement using a flag.\n\nI'll produce final code with all checks.\n\nLet's write the code in a clean way.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case['lines']\n    if not lines:\n        raise ValueError('Empty lines')\n    records = []\n    state = 'START'\n    first_violation = None\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        # parse line\n        colon = line.find(':')\n        if colon == -1:\n            first_violation = idx\n            break\n        typ = line[:colon].strip()\n        content = line[colon+1:].strip()\n        valid_types = {'Thought', 'Action', 'Action Input', 'Observation', 'Final'}\n        if typ not in valid_types:\n            first_violation = idx\n            break\n        # state machine\n        if state == 'START':\n            if typ == 'Thought':\n                state = 'THOUGHT'\n                records.append({'type': typ.lower(), 'content': content})\n            else:\n                first_violation = idx\n                break\n        elif state == 'THOUGHT':\n            if typ == 'Action':\n                state = 'ACTION'\n                records.append({'type': 'action', 'content': content})\n            else:\n                first_violation = idx\n                break\n        elif state == 'ACTION':\n            if typ == 'Action Input':\n                state = 'ACTION_INPUT'\n                records.append({'type': 'action_input', 'content': content})\n            elif typ == 'Observation':\n                state = 'OBSERVATION'\n                records.append({'type': 'observation', 'content': content})\n            else:\n                first_violation = idx\n                break\n        elif state == 'ACTION_INPUT':\n            if typ == 'Observation':\n                state = 'OBSERVATION'\n                records.append({'type': 'observation', 'content': content})\n            else:\n                first_violation = idx\n                break\n        elif state == 'OBSERVATION':\n            if typ == 'Thought':\n                state = 'THOUGHT'\n                records.append({'type': 'thought', 'content': content})\n            elif typ == 'Final':\n                state = 'FINAL'\n                records.append({'type': 'final', 'content': content})\n                # check if this is the last line later\n                final_line = idx\n            else:\n                first_violation = idx\n                break\n        elif state == 'FINAL':\n            # should only happen if we already had Final, but we would have break from seen_final? Actually we haven't tracked seen_final. We need to prevent another line after Final.\n            first_violation = idx\n            break\n        else:\n            first_violation = idx\n            break\n        # If we are in FINAL and there is a next line, that's a violation.\n        # We'll handle by checking after loop.\n    # After loop\n    if first_violation is not None:\n        # compute valid prefix up to violation line - 1\n        valid_prefix = records[:first_violation - 1] if first_violation > 1 else []\n        return {\n            'valid': False,\n            'records': records,\n            'first_violation_line': first_violation,\n            'valid_prefix': valid_prefix\n        }\n    else:\n        # No line-by-line violation. Check final state and Final position.\n        if state == 'FINAL':\n            # Final was the last line, that's good.\n            return {\n                'valid': True,\n                'records': records,\n                'first_violation_line': None,\n                'valid_prefix': records\n            }\n        elif state == 'OBSERVATION':\n            # Ended after Observation, which is fine (can end after cycle).\n            return {\n                'valid': True,\n                'records': records,\n                'first_violation_line': None,\n                'valid_prefix': records\n            }\n        else:\n            # Incomplete cycle: state is THOUGHT, ACTION, or ACTION_INPUT.\n            # Also if final_line was set but not last line, we already caught that because we would have had a violation in loop? Actually if Final appeared and then more lines, after processing Final we set state=FINAL, and then the next line would be processed: state is FINAL, so it would go into elif state=='FINAL' branch and set first_violation. So that case is already covered. So here we only have incomplete cycles not due to Final.\n            # The last line is the one that caused state to be THOUGHT/ACTION/ACTION_INPUT.\n            # That line was appended to records. The violation is that the cycle is incomplete.\n            # So we need to set first_violation_line = len(lines) (the last line), and valid_prefix = records[:-1] (exclude the incomplete last record).\n            last_line = len(lines)\n            valid_prefix = records[:-1]\n            return {\n                'valid': False,\n                'records': records,\n                'first_violation_line': last_line,\n                'valid_prefix': valid_prefix\n            }\n\nBut careful: In the loop, when we process a line that is valid and changes state to THOUGHT, ACTION, etc., we append to records. So if the loop finishes without break, the last line is included. If the final state is not OBSERVATION or FINAL, then the last record is incomplete. So we set violation at last_line and exclude it from valid_prefix.\n\nAlso, note that if the input ends with Final but it was not the last l